const title = "Whirlpool Oven Not Heating? Causes & Fix";
const description = "Whirlpool oven won't heat? On gas it's usually a weak igniter that glows but won't open the valve; on electric, the bake element. Same-day Los Angeles, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Whirlpool oven not heating?",
    a: "It depends on gas or electric. On a gas Whirlpool oven — Whirlpool builds a lot of them — the most common no-heat cause is a weak igniter: it glows but doesn't get hot enough to open the safety gas valve, so the oven never lights. On an electric Whirlpool it's usually a burned-out bake element. Both types can also be stopped by a thermal fuse blown during a self-clean cycle, a failed oven temperature sensor, or a control board that won't switch the heat on. The same parts and behavior apply to the Maytag, KitchenAid, and Amana ovens built on the Whirlpool platform. You can safely rule out settings and watch whether a gas igniter glows; the repairs themselves are technician work. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "My gas Whirlpool oven igniter glows but the oven doesn't light — why?",
    a: "That's the single most common gas-oven no-heat fault, and it's counterintuitive: the igniter is failing even though it still glows. In a gas oven the igniter does two jobs — it gets hot to light the gas, and it draws enough electrical current to open the safety gas valve. As it ages it weakens: it still glows orange, but it no longer draws enough current to open the valve, so gas never releases and the oven stays cold. So 'the igniter glows, it must be fine' is a trap — a glowing-but-weak igniter is exactly the classic failure. A technician measures the current draw and replaces the igniter; don't attempt gas-side work yourself."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I know if it's the bake element on an electric Whirlpool?",
    a: "The bake element is the loop across the bottom of the oven cavity. On bake, a healthy element glows bright orange evenly along its whole length. Signs it's failed: a visible break or split, a blistered spot, or a section that stays dark. If the oven runs but never heats and the element never glows, the element (or the control switching it) is the suspect. The visual check is safe with the oven off and cool, but the element carries 240 volts, so testing and replacing it is a technician job. If the element looks fine and still won't heat, the control board or a relay may not be switching it on."
  },
  {
    q: "My Whirlpool oven died after a self-clean — is that related?",
    a: "Very likely. Self-clean drives the oven extremely hot, and that heat commonly blows the thermal fuse — a safety that cuts power when temperatures get too high — leaving the oven dead or unable to heat afterward, and it can stress the control board too. If your Whirlpool stopped heating right after a self-clean cycle, tell us; it points straight at the thermal fuse and board. It's a known enough pattern that we're cautious recommending self-clean on older ovens. The fuse is inexpensive, but replacing it is a technician job, and we check why it blew."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Whirlpool oven that won't heat myself?",
    a: "Only the safe rule-outs. Confirm you're on Bake (not delay-start, timer, or Sabbath mode) and that a self-clean isn't still latched and cooling. On a gas model, you can watch whether the igniter glows when you call for heat — but do not touch the igniter, valve, or gas line. On an electric model, look at the bake element for a break or blister with the oven cool, and note if it heats weakly (a lost 240-volt leg). What to leave to a tech: the gas igniter and valve, the 240-volt bake element, the temperature sensor, the thermal fuse, and the control board. Let the oven cool before looking inside."
  }
];

