<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Gas or electric decides it: a weak igniter, or a burned-out element.</h2>

    <p><strong>Whirlpool builds a lot of both gas and electric ovens, and the type points at the fault. On a gas Whirlpool, the most common no-heat cause is a weak igniter — it still glows, but it can't draw enough current to open the safety gas valve, so the oven never lights. On an electric Whirlpool, it's usually a burned-out bake element. Both types can also be stopped by a thermal fuse blown during a self-clean cycle, a failed temperature sensor, or a control board that won't switch the heat on. The same parts apply to the Maytag, KitchenAid, and Amana ovens on the Whirlpool platform. You can safely check your settings, watch whether a gas igniter glows, and look at an electric element (cool) — but the repairs are technician work, and the gas side is strictly a pro job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Igniter glows but won't light?</strong> That's not proof it's fine — a glowing-but-weak igniter is the classic gas no-heat failure. More below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 GAS IGNITER -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The gas igniter — glowing isn't the same as working</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The counterintuitive #1 gas no-heat fault.</h2>

    <p>On a gas Whirlpool oven the igniter does two jobs at once: it gets hot enough to light the gas, and it draws enough electrical current to open the safety gas valve. Those are linked — the valve only opens once the igniter pulls its rated current, which is the design's way of making sure gas never releases without a flame to light it.</p>

    <p>As an igniter ages, it weakens. Here's the trap: <strong>it can still glow bright orange while no longer drawing enough current to open the valve.</strong> So the oven looks like it's trying — you see the glow — but no gas ever releases and it stays cold. "The igniter glows, so it must be fine" is exactly backwards; a glowing-but-weak igniter is the most common gas-oven no-heat failure. A technician measures the actual current draw against spec and replaces the igniter. Never work on the igniter, valve, or gas line yourself.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Whirlpool oven won't heat</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Weak gas igniter (gas models, most common)</h3>
      <p>Glows but can't draw enough current to open the gas valve, so the oven never lights. The classic gas no-heat fault. Tech measures current draw and replaces — gas side is pro-only.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Bake element (electric models)</h3>
      <p>Burned out — visible break, blister, or dark section. The electric equivalent. 240-volt tech replacement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermal fuse (often after self-clean)</h3>
      <p>A safety that cuts power on overheat. Self-clean's extreme heat commonly blows it, leaving the oven dead or unable to heat. Inexpensive part, tech replacement — and we check why it blew.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Oven temperature sensor</h3>
      <p>Out of spec, it can stop the oven calling for heat or heat to the wrong temperature. Tech tests resistance against spec.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Control board / relay</h3>
      <p>If the board or a relay won't energize the igniter or element, the oven won't heat even with good parts. Tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rule these out before it's a repair.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Confirm the mode</strong> — you're on Bake, not a delay-start, timer, or Sabbath mode, and no self-clean is still latched and cooling.</li>
      <li><strong>Gas model: watch for the glow.</strong> When you call for heat, does the igniter glow? A glow with no flame points at a weak igniter — but don't touch it.</li>
      <li><strong>Electric model: look at the bake element (cool)</strong> for a visible break, blister, or dark spot.</li>
      <li><strong>Note the timing</strong> — if it died right after a self-clean, say so; that points at the thermal fuse.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> checking the mode, watching the igniter glow, and a cool visual of an electric element are safe. The gas igniter and valve, the 240-volt element, the sensor, the thermal fuse, and the control board are our techs' work — and never touch the gas line.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 PLATFORM -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Same platform: Maytag, KitchenAid, Amana</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">One oven design, several badges.</h2>

    <p>Because Whirlpool builds Maytag, KitchenAid, and Amana ovens on the same platform, the gas igniter, bake element, thermal fuse, and control behave the same across all of them — so a Maytag or KitchenAid oven that won't heat is diagnosed exactly like a Whirlpool. Our techs carry the common Whirlpool-platform igniters, elements, and fuses that fit the family, so it's usually a one-visit fix. If your oven shows a fault code instead, our <a href="/services/oven-repair/error-codes/">oven error codes</a> guide covers what the common ones mean.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Igniter glows but won't light, or element looks bad?</h2>

    <p>Either way it's a diagnostic. On a gas Whirlpool we measure the igniter's current draw and replace it if it's weak; on electric we test the element, and if it looks fine we check the sensor, thermal fuse, board, and relays with a meter. Our techs handle gas and electric Whirlpool ovens (and the Maytag/KitchenAid/Amana equivalents) and carry the common parts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/oven-repair/not-heating/">oven not heating</a> guide, the same fault on <a href="/services/oven-repair/samsung-not-heating/">Samsung ovens</a>, the <a href="/services/oven-repair/error-codes/">oven error codes</a> page, and <a href="/services/oven-repair/temperature-off/">oven temperature off</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/oven-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/oven-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/oven-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool oven not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
